VIOLATION — MUST-3: a `guard` must sit at a tool-axis event AND name the tools that can
actually PERFORM the thing it inspects. This guard names all nine tools in the roster.

The fence inspects WRITES. Read, Grep, Glob and WebFetch cannot write a file, so the fence
can never have anything to say about a call to any of them — yet each one pays hook-process
startup on every invocation. The transcript prices it in its own last command: 611
invocations of the fence in one day's log, against a subject that can only ever arise on a
mutation.

THE LOAD-BEARING SHAPE — the matcher is a NAMED LIST, and every mechanical arm is green
because of it. This rule's structural detector FAILs a `guard` carrying a `*` matcher and
FAILs one carrying NO matcher; it has no arm for a matcher that enumerates the roster,
because `Edit|Write|NotebookEdit|Read|Grep|Glob|Bash|Task|WebFetch` is neither of those. The
transcript shows the validator saying so in as many words —
`matcher: named, not "*", not absent` — and PASSing. A check reading the registration alone
scores this clean. Only comparing the enumerated tools against what the hook INSPECTS
separates the poles. Enumerating the whole roster is `*` written the long way: same blast
radius, same startup cost, and it reaches them while satisfying the letter of the narrowing
requirement.

The session's own reasoning is the tell. It derives the list from `tool-names.js::ALL` — the
roster of everything that exists — rather than from the set of tools that can write. It
never asks which of the nine can perform a write; it asks which tools exist.

Discriminator 1 — MUST-1 IS SATISFIED. One marker for one registration, event and class
tokens in the vocabulary, rationale non-empty and truthful about why `PreToolUse` can see
the subject: it refuses the write at the boundary that would apply it, before anything is
written. That sentence is correct and is this hook's own.

Discriminator 2 — MUST-2 IS NOT ENGAGED. The class is `guard`, not `verification`, and the
event is `PreToolUse`, not `SessionStart`. The class is also the RIGHT one — the subject is
one specific action at the boundary attempting it — so there is no mislabel to find.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-3's FIRST arm is SATISFIED. The guard does sit at a tool-axis event.
`PreToolUse` is one of the two events the rule permits for a narrow class, and the choice is
argued from the need to refuse before the write lands. Only the second arm, the narrowing,
fails.

Discriminator 4 — MUST-4 IS SATISFIED. The declared `(event, matcher)` set equals the
registered set, character for character across nine tool names, and the transcript shows the
comparison passing. The marker and `settings.json` agree exactly — about a matcher that is
too wide in both places.

Discriminator 5 — MUST NOT bullet 1 IS NOT ENGAGED. The event was chosen from the subject,
not from where other hooks sit or from which event is easiest to fire. MUST NOT bullet 2 is
not engaged either: no rule's Detection mechanism cites this hook in the excerpt.

Discriminator 6 — the fence's CONTAINMENT LOGIC is sound and is not the finding. It resolves
both the candidate and the lane root through `realpathSync` and compares canonical forms,
which is the correct construction; a judge reporting a path-containment defect has named
something this excerpt does not contain.

The whole defect is a matcher that lists every tool that exists instead of every tool that
can write, with every structural arm green because a list is not a wildcard.
